WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.429
La Llorona, known as the “The Weeping”
or “Wailing Woman” was likely born in

00:00:03.429 --> 00:00:04.620
the 1650s.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.879
We know this because she appears to be in
her twenties when she is married with children

00:00:07.879 --> 00:00:08.879
in 1673.

00:00:08.879 --> 00:00:13.600
La Llorona was alive so long ago that the
best known record of her mostly comes from

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.010
the folklore surrounding her.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:18.110
While the stories vary, depending on the source,
a few constants overlap.

00:00:18.110 --> 00:00:20.810
When La Llorona was young, she had a different
name.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:25.917
In some retellings, it’s Maria but in a
well known play written in 1917, it’s Luisa.

00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:29.970
In many iterations of the story, she was born
into poverty but as she grew up she became

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.380
incredibly beautiful.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.289
So much so, that she drew the attention of
a wealthy ranchero.

00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:36.120
He married her and they had two boys.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.719
Unfortunately these children would not live
for very long, as she gave them live and she

00:00:40.719 --> 00:00:44.079
took it away, thus beginning the curse of
La Llorona.

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:48.269
To find out what drove this young mother to
commit this act, how she became known as the

00:00:48.269 --> 00:00:53.100
weeping woman, and how her curse endangered
generations of children, stick around to the

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:54.217
end of this video.

00:00:54.217 --> 00:00:55.801
This video is sponsored by Audible.

00:00:55.801 --> 00:00:59.772
(metal music)

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:06.289
Welcome to Horror History – In today’s
lesson, I’ll be covering the life, and afterlife,

00:01:06.289 --> 00:01:10.110
of one of Mexico and Central America’s most
iconic legends, La Llorona.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:14.470
There’s not a lot documented about the childhood
of La Llorona, but one thing that is consistent

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:18.680
across the various legends is that she grew
up in poverty, so when a wealthy conquistador

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:22.280
came to her town and pursued her, it makes
sense that this would be a very big deal to

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:23.280
her.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.510
According to History Today, there was initially
a shortage of Spanish women in what the Spanish

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:30.530
dubbed as “New Spain”, the region that is
now referred to as “Spring Break”.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:34.530
This meant that unions between indigenous
women and Spanish men were quite common.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:37.530
However many of these unions didn’t last
very long.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:41.490
To get a better understanding, we’ll need
to take it back to Mexico and parts of Central

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:45.151
America during the 1600s where this iteration
of the legend originates.

00:01:45.151 --> 00:01:48.322
(spanish guitar music)

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:54.460
“The fates of indigenous and mestiza women
who married Spaniards were mixed.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.859
Some enjoyed stability and enhanced status.

00:01:56.859 --> 00:02:01.451
But more often than not, they were cast aside
after a few years for younger women or, more

00:02:01.451 --> 00:02:03.060
often, a Spanish wife.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.920
More alarmingly, their children were sometimes
taken away, a practice that derived from the

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:11.090
Spanish tradition of relieving so-called ‘wayward’
women of their children.”

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:14.720
This left many women during this time left
to feel completely helpless – La Llorona

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:18.731
is kind of an embodiment of the collective
pain and anger of women from this time, that

00:02:18.731 --> 00:02:19.930
got turned into legend.

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:23.470
It’s something that’s come up a lot in
Horror History, from Godzilla being a metaphor

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:28.300
for nuclear fallout to The Demogorgon being
a manifestation of fears of communism.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.910
I think we need a term for this so I’ll
call it…

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:32.170
Cultural Legendizing.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:33.520
That sounds pretty good.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.810
There are different iterations of La Llorona,
but for the sake of Horror History, we’ll

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:41.250
stick to the lore as told in The Conjuring
universe, more specifically, the 2019 film

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.940
The Curse Of La Llorona.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:46.811
According to the local Priest, Father Perez,
the story goes as follows: La Llorona fell

00:02:46.811 --> 00:02:48.900
in love and had two sons with her husband.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.120
The family loved each other, and her sons
demonstrated their care for her by giving

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.349
her a necklace:

00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:55.340
– “I will wear this forever”.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:59.290
– I don’t think she knew at the time how literally
true this statement was.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:02.790
Father Perez explains that she and her family
were happy until one day when she found her

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:04.700
man in the arms of a younger woman.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:08.680
In a fit of rage, she took away what he deemed
the most valuable, his testicles.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:12.700
Oh wait, no, it was his children, and she
drowned them in the nearby river.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:16.530
As soon as her rageblindness cleared and she
realized what she’d done, she weeped and

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:20.390
then took her own life in the same river,
cursing herself to an eternity of trying to

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:23.000
find souls of children to trade for her own.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:31.230
– Children are told to behave, listen to
their elders, or La Llorona will come and

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.250
steal them away.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.930
– It kind of seems like the moral of the story
has been aimed at the wrong people.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:39.680
The kids are the only people who didn’t
do anything wrong and they suffered the most,

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.340
and future generations of children are made
to fear the tale La Llorona, when it should

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.000
probably be directed at couples.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.920
For the next one hundred years, La Llorona
continued to haunt the river and other bodies

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.090
of water, searching for children to kill.

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:54.200
As the legend of grew, people claimed to hear
a woman crying.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.010
Some followed the sounds to find a woman in
a white gown floating above the water.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:01.570
If children were the ones to find her, she
was said to be ruthless and they wouldn’t

00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:02.930
stand a chance of escaping.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:06.740
These stories mostly come from Mexico, but
like my friends at the nearby taco truck,

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:09.100
the legend would burrow its way across the
border.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:12.250
In 1973 the weeping appeared in Los Angeles.

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:16.250
All this time I thought it was La Llorona;
maybe it’s actually L.A. Llorona.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:20.560
It’s never clearly explained how La Llorona
went from haunting one specific river in Mexico

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:24.630
to becoming an Angelino, we just know she’s
doomed to an eternity of searching rivers

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:25.780
and bodies of water.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:29.780
There are several rivers, such as the Colorado
River or the Tijuana River, that run from

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.720
Mexico into Southern California.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.860
So over the course of a hundred years, she
could have migrated up these channels.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.360
While La Llorona was just a children’s tale
to some, the single mother, Patricia Alvarez

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.590
and the recent widow, Anna Garcia, were about
to experience the curse first hand.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:47.590
I think La Llorona targeted these two women
because they are reminiscent of herself.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:51.710
Patricia is alone raising her kids and based
on Anna’s questions about if she’s started

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:55.140
drinking again, it is clear that she’s had
a rough life and doesn’t have any support

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:56.140
system.

00:04:56.140 --> 00:04:59.330
Anna lost her husband in the line of fire
and while he didn’t choose to leave her,

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:02.010
she was still left in a difficult situation
nonetheless.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:06.090
Both Patricia and Anna have two children close
in age to La Llorona’s two kids, and both

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:07.090
are hispanic.

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:10.850
La Llorona first targets Patricia and her
two sons, Carlos and Tomas.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:15.080
Patricia seems to take all the necessary steps
to deter the malicious spirit: a shrine of

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:19.220
candles, boarded up windows, religious iconography,
and a closet transformed into a protective

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:20.880
spirit shelter for her kids.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:24.139
She may have set this up based on her own
knowledge of the legend of La Llorona, which

00:05:24.139 --> 00:05:26.590
she demonstrates a pretty clear understanding
of:

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:27.820
– [Patricia] Your children.

00:05:27.820 --> 00:05:30.000
Have they heard her crying?

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.020
Have they felt the sting of her tears?

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:35.500
They will.

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:36.750
Soon.

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:41.090
– Or she could have received guidance from Rafael,
a currendaro or healer, who is an expert at

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:42.390
handling La Llorona.

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:46.240
This might explain why Rafael is at the service
for the two boys after they are killed and

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.620
why he goes back later to collect a sample
of La Llorona’s tears.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:53.620
Either way, when Anna arrives and finds that
Patricia has hidden her children away, Patricia

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:57.840
begs Anna for “just one more night”, which
seems to imply that if La Llorona isn’t

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:00.960
able to take the children within a certain
time frame, she moves on.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.590
This could be representative of the nature
by which La Llorona killed her own children.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:08.780
It happened in a fit of rage and once she
had calmed down, she regretted her choice.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:13.060
So maybe, if given enough time, she will spare
the children she’s after, just as she likely

00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:14.340
would have with her own.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.050
But that's just a theory.

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.050
A FILM THEO–

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:20.230
In the Conjerverse, La Llorona does not just
kill children upon first sight.

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:23.150
Her haunting follows a specific process, which
we’ve seen before.

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:26.800
Her curse seems to follow the same rules that
apply to demonic possession, by following

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.360
the steps of “infestation,” “oppression”
and then finally, “possession.”

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:34.710
In La Llorona’s case, she isn’t necessarily
trying to possess a person in “take-over-their-body”

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:37.830
the sense that we’re used to seeing, but
she is trying to possess childrens’ souls

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:40.280
in order to try to bring her own children
back to her.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:43.940
It makes sense that La Llorona’s curse follows
the same rules as demonic possession, just

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:47.160
as Bathsheba did with the Perron family back
in 1971.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.730
Bathsheba is not technically a demon, but
the terror she dishes out follows the same

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:51.730
process.

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:55.340
But La Llorona’s version of infestation,
oppression and possession have unique ways

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:59.467
of manifesting themselves, which creates unique
problems for all those involved.

